Part A - Data Cleaning Decicions:

  1. The most unexpected data quality that I found was when I was attempting merge the FitZone_Products_Reference table to the products table, the In-Store Transactions table, and the Online Orders table, I found that I was having issues with obtaining the correct values needed, which led to some frustration on my end when I was a little bit further along in the project with everything because I was trying to wrap my head around why the data didn't look the way I needed it to. I ended up figuring out that I needed to trim and clean the values before obtaining the values I needed. 

  2. My thought process for getting rid of, or filtering out, the negative values is that those represent items that have been bought by our customers and then returned to the store within the appropriate time frame. The reason in which I came to this conclusion is because of my own background in retail, which informed me that when you see a negative value within the system that means the customer was not satisfied with the product that was purchased and they decided to return it. This decision might impact the visuals because it won't show the full picture of the data, but it did not make the most sense to me at the time to keep negative values that represented returns/refunds since those items are back with the company. 

  3. For In-Store Sales and Online-Sales, the only challenge I faced with those was in a later section when I needed to combine their data for one pie/donut chart, but overall there weren't any issues with them at all. 

